#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563954 :question/problem "![](images/a6/a688cf79790cde240a1fabeb768a9500c98e7affc1006c39e35b89582627fb0c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the elliptic arc is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc2f364085ccca85e6e6ddd8a5affac51c84f774281b817d4852bd2322bcb1b.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ the force is still perpendicular to the motion.\n\n![](images/50/508e0ac5f67d7b119ad689ccaede8894268eefe31d9b00f148ddd6bf851de6b4.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564467 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ 2x-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge {x}^{2} \\\\ y\\ne 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564477 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,y<x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $>$ sign, the line is not part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a dashed line $y=x$ and shade the area *below* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/77/77a1a03d037ee571fb0dc14ec8370c8b01f521b5a54798117c0b131000676599.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564488 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564498 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡y}{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564504 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ x-y\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564505 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564508 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}$ at the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,-2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} & =\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-2)-2}{{2}^{3}-(-2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-2}{8-4-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564511 :question/problem "Evaluate $z={2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}$ at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,2)} & =({2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}){|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & ={2}^{-2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564512 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564517 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $<$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1$ and shade the region *inside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/59/59ac983361be7dd9a991e1cf134e7193256891a5587eab6bff5edf906fef3688.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564520 :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1).\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-3,1),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3,1) & =x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1)\\,{|}_{(-3,1)} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1+1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564533 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nNotice that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x.$ Therefore, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564534 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<x<1 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564538 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0\n$$\n\nSince the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0$ is true for all values of $x$ and $y,$ the domain of the function $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564601 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${120}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564606 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${210}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={210}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({210}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({210}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564611 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2\\sqrt{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${300}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=2\\sqrt{3},\\,\\theta ={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{3},-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564617 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564631 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2\\sqrt{3},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=2\\sqrt{3},\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564633 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5\\sqrt{3}$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{2π}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=5\\sqrt{3},\\,\\theta =\\frac{2π}{3}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2π}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{15}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564636 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564648 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $4\\sqrt{2},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=4\\sqrt{2},\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564649 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $5\\sqrt{2},$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=5\\sqrt{2},\\,\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(5,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564650 :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $1,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${45}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564664 :question/problem "![](images/42/42e881f2fea91ee9191c5b32dce49f485977ded98a9e900bfcaf51fc1e0f8ec1.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/49/494158fe124fe1c8e1f7f618695dc9846991bd074b199a45a5874ddb8ad16e86.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{17}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564668 :question/problem "![](images/00/00c1e8199146ddfdc1d8243eccfc5d5f44f9e67474722365a5c39c4a2226dcab.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/a0/a0fbd1bc9dfacd406a027a0178d223dc972379a6b370b121f28975c345696f85.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{\\frac{8}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2+\\frac{10}{3}+2=\\frac{22}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564670 :question/problem "![](images/69/69ff60a180694c898c1ae208d6e2dd95ef82ec0f27b7e4c1cc0256a04ae696b9.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $6$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/96/964e6b51949b7162cfcd71f047b85980ef6387fb8268865a93b38183c060e844.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a semi-circle of radius $r=6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\pi r=6 \\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $12.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=12.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=6 \\pi+12=6(\\pi+2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564672 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ae1eeb848f8d01597ec509c095d8a0307805640690d9d65102909e2b861e5d2.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1e/1ec516fa1aa0172ba521d53fc11abd6268cac8eda4d1f221b741d5d9049dda99.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{13}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{13 \\pi}{6} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{13}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{13}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{13}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{13 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{13 \\pi}{6}+\\frac{13 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{13}{6}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564673 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1aad44e8db3ab56b858a003aada2ea280b879365de13842d7a41f9d7f89e822f.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/02/022aa19e71aed46d212400b6e403406fe3d21a657bd5b45eb23f00fbd32156eb.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is three-quarters of a circle of radius $r=11.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{3}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{33 \\pi}{2} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $11.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{33 \\pi}{2}+11.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564679 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e028416a02722d1d832c2daddddbb23a314694ddd328670ed58376681dc7c023.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/5d/5d932a90da98448134bdb2deb9b524636ef7ad9aa59a7e91f9fe0d75178f395e.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{16}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{8 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{16}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{16}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{16}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{8 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564699 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6128159e1606c72588f4881db5156ec3b73be224c160c15a0977a1eb1f824fa.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/2e/2e4d02399788c449b02d77757c66eb3be78e38289f0af5eac3ae784b5d0f2f5d.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $8$ and $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{6}^{2}}=10.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $1$ and $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{6}^{2}}=\\sqrt{37}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=10+\\sqrt{37}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564707 :question/problem "![](images/26/26b263723ce79bb4ddb59ceac1f179447430b3cf8c3d14e3ea0dfc7612fc0665.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/55/553abb9d23d0a9e5f6b980e0c6418404881db984b6180caab1f0624d2deb9397.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $8.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $8$ and $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{6}^{2}}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=8+10+8=26.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564717 :question/problem "![](images/35/35cb0f44f9b2b88a56351a295c9b1d4de93674d10d914c3017e8fd9382d1e446.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $11$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/bd/bdc6176ad2a20edab8ce185794c48dcb426828f561bceadddc3b3cb2e693fb62.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a semi-circle of radius $r=11.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\pi r=11 \\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $22.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=11 \\pi+22=11(\\pi+2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564724 :question/problem "![](images/24/249efb9fbaa6cc4f1bd923dd5aaa93d149cc07c56e23eb8ba12870b16c9108d3.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/20/20f9c299762ace89c0d0f8db158d6c8cedf266c5a08ad580c314a7d964897596.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $6$ and $\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}}=\\frac{13}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=6+\\frac{13}{2}+6=\\frac{37}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565416 :question/problem "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x+2}{1-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =\\frac{3(0)+2}{1-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the $-1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3x+2}{1-3y} & =-1 \\\\ 3x+2 & =-(1-3y) \\\\ 3x+2 & =3y-1 \\\\ 3y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(0,1)$ is the line $y=x+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565424 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565425 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565428 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $H$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3},5) & =\\frac{6}{5-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{6}{y-{x}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 2 & =y-{x}^{2} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $H(\\sqrt{3},5)$ is the parabola $y={x}^{2}+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565429 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $F$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{7},1) & =\\frac{12(1)}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-{1}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 6y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+9 & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $F(\\sqrt{7},1)$ is the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}]}
